            "createdOn": 1751882561,
            "author": {
                "name": "Howard Chang",
                "email": "howard.chang.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                "username": "Howard-wiwynn"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Why does it _need_ to contain timestamps?  Shouldn't the host OS have its own timestamps?\n\nAt a minimum this is going to need to be an option, I suspect."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Howard Chang",
                        "email": "howard.chang.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                        "username": "Howard-wiwynn"
                    },
                    "message": "We use this feature to monitor boot time in the fleet. Currently, this is not a requirement for any phase exit, but would be nice to have as early in NPI as possible to monitor the effect of different components and firmwares on boot time to be sure that we will be within our maintenance SLOs during production."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Howard Chang",
                        "email": "howard.chang.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                        "username": "Howard-wiwynn"
                    },
                    "message": "If the BIOS POST code is stuck (or looping), having a timestamp allows us to determine the exact time it occurred through the console log, providing more cross-reference points in terms of timing during debugging."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Howard Chang",
                        "email": "howard.chang.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                        "username": "Howard-wiwynn"
                    },
                    "message": "I think it would be a better approach to make all logs written by obmc-console have the same timestamp format.\n\nThis will make future maintenance easier. Based on current testing, special characters (such as colors, cursor movements, etc.) still retain their original formatting."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jeremy Kerr",
                        "email": "jk@ozlabs.org",
                        "username": "jk-ozlabs"
                    },
                    "message": "The serial console is not a stream of ASCII lines; it's essentially binary data, that *most of the time* is line-based.\n\nWhat happens when a tui is started on the console? How are you intending to handle the tty control sequences? Inserting timestamps into that binary stream is going to make things messy..."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Howard Chang",
                        "email": "howard.chang.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                        "username": "Howard-wiwynn"
                    },
                    "message": "What happens when a tui is started on the console? \nIt will print the timestamp at the beginning and leave the rest unchanged.\n\nHow are you intending to handle the tty control sequences?\nControl characters are still displayed in the same way as before, including colors, line breaks, cursor movements, and so on.\n\nIndeed, if the input contains control characters like '\\r', which moves the cursor back to the beginning of the line, using $cat to view the file may result in the timestamp being overwritten. However, if you open the file with $vi (which does not interpret control characters), the timestamp will be displayed correctly."
                }
            ],
